.TH "ARI" "1" "October 2026" "ari 0.6.1" "User Commands"
.hy
.SH NAME
.PP
ari - keep SSH hosts in one place and
generate ssh config and Ansible
inventory from them
.SH SYNOPSIS
.PP
\f[B]ari\f[R] [\f[B]-i\f[R]
\f[I]INV\f[R]]
.PP
\f[B]ari\f[R] [\f[B]-i\f[R]
\f[I]INV\f[R]] \f[I]COMMAND\f[R]
[\f[I]ARGS\f[R]]
.PP
\f[B]ari init\f[R]
.PP
\f[B]ari ls\f[R] [\f[B]--search\f[R]
\f[I]TEXT\f[R]] [\f[B]--group\f[R]
\f[I]GROUP\f[R]]
.PP
\f[B]ari show\f[R] \f[I]NAME\f[R]
.PP
\f[B]ari add\f[R] \f[I]NAME\f[R]
\f[I]HOSTNAME\f[R] [\f[I]OPTIONS\f[R]]
.PP
\f[B]ari edit\f[R] \f[I]NAME\f[R]
[\f[I]OPTIONS\f[R]]
.PP
\f[B]ari rm\f[R] \f[I]NAME\f[R]
.PP
\f[B]ari group\f[R] [\f[I]NAME\f[R]
[\f[I]OPTIONS\f[R]]]
.PP
\f[B]ari modules\f[R]
.PP
\f[B]ari import\f[R] \f[I]MODULE\f[R]
[\f[I]SOURCE\f[R]] [\f[B]--exclude\f[R]
\f[I]MODULE\f[R]]
.PP
\f[B]ari export\f[R] [\f[I]MODULE\f[R]
\&...]
[\f[B]--force\f[R]]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.PP
\f[B]ari\f[R] keeps one record per SSH
host in JSON inventories and generates
files from them through modules.
Each inventory, such as
\f[I]personal\f[R] or \f[I]work\f[R], is
declared in \f[B]config.toml\f[R] with
the modules it uses.
The built-in \f[B]ssh\f[R] module
imports and writes OpenSSH client
config; the built-in \f[B]ansible\f[R]
module imports and writes an Ansible
YAML inventory.
.PP
Group membership lives on the host
record, so removing a host removes it
from every group, and no group can name
a host that doesn\[aq]t exist.
.PP
Names and aliases are unique across all
inventories, compared case-insensitively
the way ssh compares them.
Every generated file lands in one ssh
namespace where the first match wins, so
a duplicate would silently shadow a
host.
.PP
Generated files are output, not
something to edit.
\f[B]ari\f[R] records a hash of every
file it writes and refuses to overwrite
one that changed since.
.PP
With no command on a terminal,
\f[B]ari\f[R] opens the TUI, described
under \f[B]TUI\f[R].
When input or output isn\[aq]t a
terminal, it prints help instead.
.SH THE NAME
.PP
\f[B]ari\f[R] is Turkish for bee.
Bees spend their days building the hive
and stocking it, and that is the whole
job here: keep every host in one place,
and build ssh config and Ansible
inventory from it.
.PP
The name started with a plush hive.
My son has one with five bees, and he
loves it.
One of the bees lives on my desk.
.PP
In Hebrew, ari means lion.
When Samson came back to the lion he had
killed, he found bees and honey inside
it:
.RS
.PP
And after a time he returned to take
her, and he turned aside to see the
carcase of the lion: and, behold, there
was a swarm of bees and honey in the
carcase of the lion.
.PP
Judges 14:8 (KJV)
.RE
.PP
Out of the strong came forth sweetness.
.SH COMMANDS
.SS init
.PP
Write a starter \f[B]config.toml\f[R]
and print where it went.
The starter declares one inventory,
\f[I]personal\f[R], with the
\f[B]ssh\f[R] module on, and carries a
second, \f[I]work\f[R], commented out
with an \f[B]ansible\f[R] table to
adapt.
When config.toml exists, whatever it
holds, \f[B]init\f[R] refuses and leaves
it alone.
.SS ls
.PP
List hosts across every inventory, or
only the one named with \f[B]-i\f[R].
User and port show effective values,
with inventory defaults filled in.
.PP
Between PORT and GROUPS, each module
that writes files for an inventory
listed gets a column, headed by its
name: \f[B]✓\f[R] when \f[B]export\f[R]
writes the host there,
\f[I]excluded\f[R] when the host lists
that module in \f[B]exclude\f[R], and
\f[B]·\f[R] when the host\[aq]s
inventory doesn\[aq]t have the module
on.
A parked module gets no column.
.PP
GROUPS shows as many of a host\[aq]s
groups as fit beside the other columns,
in the order the host lists them, then
+\f[I]N\f[R] for the rest, so a row
never wraps on their account;
\f[B]show\f[R] lists them all.
Output to a pipe is laid out for 80
columns unless \f[B]COLUMNS\f[R] says
otherwise.
The other columns are never cut: when
they alone don\[aq]t fit, GROUPS shrinks
to the width of its heading and the rows
run past the edge, every name and
address whole.
.PP
.TS
tab(@);
lw(24.5n) lw(45.5n).
