.TE
.SS edit \f[I]NAME\f[R]
.PP
Change a host, found by name or alias.
\f[B]-i\f[R] narrows the search to one
inventory.
Takes every \f[B]add\f[R] option, plus
these:
.PP
.TS
tab(@);
lw(24.5n) lw(45.5n).
T{
Option
T}@T{
Meaning
T}
_
T{
\f[B]--hostname\f[R] \f[I]HOSTNAME\f[R]
T}@T{
New address
T}
T{
\f[B]--rename\f[R] \f[I]NAME\f[R]
T}@T{
New name
T}
T{
\f[B]--unalias\f[R] \f[I]ALIAS\f[R]
T}@T{
Drop an alias, matched
case-insensitively; repeatable
T}
T{
\f[B]--ungroup\f[R] \f[I]GROUP\f[R]
T}@T{
Leave a group, and its reason with it;
repeatable
T}
T{
\f[B]--include\f[R] \f[I]MODULE\f[R]
T}@T{
Undo an \f[B]--exclude\f[R]; repeatable
T}
.TE
.PP
An empty value clears a field:
\f[B]--user \[aq]\[aq]\f[R], \f[B]--port
\[aq]\[aq]\f[R] and \f[B]--key
\[aq]\[aq]\f[R] go back to the inventory
default, \f[B]--notes \[aq]\[aq]\f[R]
empties the notes, and \f[B]--opt\f[R]
\f[I]KEY\f[R]= removes that option.
\f[B]--group\f[R] sets membership
exactly as given, so naming a group the
host is already in without a reason
drops its reason.
\f[B]--unalias\f[R] runs before
\f[B]--alias\f[R], so
\f[B]--unalias\f[R] \f[I]OLD\f[R]
\f[B]--alias\f[R] \f[I]NEW\f[R] swaps
one alias for another, a change of case
included.
\f[B]--ungroup\f[R] a group the host
isn\[aq]t in, \f[B]--unalias\f[R] a name
that isn\[aq]t one of its aliases,
\f[B]--include\f[R] a module it
doesn\[aq]t exclude, and clearing an
option it doesn\[aq]t have are errors,
not silent no-ops.
An edit that changes nothing saves
nothing.
.SS rm \f[I]NAME\f[R]
.PP
Remove a host, found by name or alias,
from whichever inventory holds it.
\f[B]-i\f[R] narrows the search.
Its group memberships go with it.
.SS group [\f[I]NAME\f[R]]
.PP
Without \f[I]NAME\f[R], list the
declared groups of every inventory, or
only the one named with \f[B]-i\f[R], in
the order they\[aq]re declared.
HOSTS counts a group\[aq]s hosts as
Ansible sees them: the ones that list it
and the ones any group below it holds,
with the direct count beside it when
children bring more.
CHILDREN, REASONS and ABOUT, the
description\[aq]s first line, share the
width the other columns leave and never
wrap.
GROUP, HOSTS and INV are never cut: when
they alone don\[aq]t fit, the other
three shrink to the width of their
headings and the rows run past the edge.
.PP
With \f[I]NAME\f[R], declare that group
in one inventory, \f[B]-i\f[R], then
\f[B]ARI_INVENTORY\f[R], then
\f[B]default\f[R] from config.toml, or
change it if it\[aq]s declared already.
A new name needs at least one character
and no spaces or colons, so
\f[B]--group\f[R]
\f[I]GROUP\f[R][:\f[I]REASON\f[R]] can
name it.
.PP
.TS
tab(@);
lw(24.5n) lw(45.5n).
T{
Option
T}@T{
Meaning
T}
_
T{
\f[B]--description\f[R] \f[I]TEXT\f[R]
T}@T{
A zone\[aq]s section header in the hosts
file; further lines become comments
under it.
\f[B]\[aq]\[aq]\f[R] clears it
T}
T{
\f[B]--child\f[R] \f[I]GROUP\f[R]
T}@T{
Add a declared group as a child;
repeatable
T}
T{
\f[B]--unchild\f[R] \f[I]GROUP\f[R]
T}@T{
Drop a child; repeatable, and runs
before \f[B]--child\f[R]
T}
T{
\f[B]--reason\f[R]
\f[I]KEY\f[R]=\f[I]TEXT\f[R]
T}@T{
Add a reason hosts can give for being in
the group, or change its text;
\f[I]KEY\f[R]= removes it; repeatable
T}
T{
\f[B]--rm\f[R]
T}@T{
Remove the group; takes no other option
T}
.TE
.PP
Nothing a host relies on can go.
\f[B]--rm\f[R] is refused while any host
is in the group, directly or through a
child, or while another group lists it
as a child.
\f[B]--unchild\f[R] is refused when the
parent would lose a host it reaches only
through that child.
Removing a reason is refused while a
host gives it.
Each refusal names the hosts and how
they get there, and nothing is saved.
.PP
The other checks match \f[B]add\f[R] and
\f[B]edit\f[R]: every child is declared,
children form no cycle, and every
problem is listed at once.
Dropping a child or a reason the group
doesn\[aq]t have is an error.
A command that changes nothing saves
nothing.
\f[B]export\f[R] still applies each
module\[aq]s own rules, such as which
Ansible file a group is routed to.
.SS modules
.PP
List the installed modules, whether each
can import and export, and which
inventories switch it on.
Modules that failed to load are listed
with the reason.
Without config.toml it still lists the
modules, with no inventories.
.SS import \f[I]MODULE\f[R] [\f[I]SOURCE\f[R]]
.PP
Read hosts into one inventory through
\f[I]MODULE\f[R].
Every host goes through the same checks
as \f[B]add\f[R]: one that fails is
refused and reported, and the rest still
import.
A host already in the inventory is
merged: new aliases, groups, options and
missing notes are added.
A value the source sets differently,
whether HostName, User, Port,
IdentityFile, notes or an ssh option
such as ProxyJump, is a conflict, and
that host is left alone.
Hosts that merged are saved even when
others conflicted or were refused; the
exit status is then 1.
.PP
Importing into an empty inventory sets
its defaults: from the source\[aq]s own
defaults when it has them, like
Ansible\[aq]s \f[B]all.vars\f[R],
otherwise to the user and key most hosts
share.
Each host stores only what differs.
An inventory with hosts or defaults
keeps its own, and a host that leaves a
field to the source\[aq]s defaults takes
the source\[aq]s value, stored where it
differs from the inventory\[aq]s.
A host already in the inventory whose
value would change that way is a
conflict, and the warning names the
hosts that were given one.
.PP
A clean import adopts the files it read:
their hashes go to the guard, so
exporting over them afterwards passes.
If anything conflicted, was refused, or
couldn\[aq]t be represented, such as a
skipped \f[V]Host *\f[R] block or a
second LocalForward, the files are not
adopted, and an export over one of them
needs \f[B]--force\f[R] once you\[aq]ve
checked it.
.PP
.TS
tab(@);
lw(24.5n) lw(45.5n).
T{
Option
T}@T{
Meaning
T}
_
T{
\f[B]--exclude\f[R] \f[I]MODULE\f[R]
T}@T{
Keep the imported hosts out of
\f[I]MODULE\f[R]\[aq]s output;
repeatable
T}
.TE
.PP
Each key file the source names becomes
the inventory\[aq]s key at that path.
A path the inventory has no key for is
declared under the file\[aq]s stem,
numbered when the name is taken, and the
report lists the keys it declared.
A key only refused or conflicting hosts
would have used isn\[aq]t declared.
.PP
With the \f[B]ssh\f[R] module,
\f[I]SOURCE\f[R] is a config file.
The first token on a Host line becomes
the name and the rest become aliases.
A block without \f[B]HostName\f[R] is
read the way ssh reads it, which
connects to whichever token was typed:
each token becomes a host of its own,
connecting to its own name, with the
block\[aq]s user, port, key and options.
A block for a host the inventory already
has, a later block in the same file or
the same host on re-import, compares and
fills only what it sets, so without
\f[B]HostName\f[R] or \f[B]Port\f[R] it
can still add a user, key, alias or
option.
A new host without \f[B]Port\f[R] gets
22, ssh\[aq]s own default, stored only
when the inventory\[aq]s default port
differs.
\f[B]HostName\f[R], \f[B]User\f[R] and
\f[B]Port\f[R] become fields,
\f[B]IdentityFile\f[R] the host\[aq]s
key, and every other keyword is kept as
an ssh option, in order.
Export writes \f[B]IdentitiesOnly
yes\f[R] after a key unless the host
sets IdentitiesOnly itself, so a block
with a key and no IdentitiesOnly is
stored with \f[B]IdentitiesOnly no\f[R],
which is what ssh did with it.
Pattern Host blocks, Match blocks,
Include lines and options outside any
Host block are skipped with a warning.
When a keyword repeats inside a block,
ssh uses the first value and so does
ari, except for \f[B]IdentityFile\f[R],
\f[B]CertificateFile\f[R],
\f[B]LocalForward\f[R],
\f[B]RemoteForward\f[R],
\f[B]DynamicForward\f[R] and
\f[B]SendEnv\f[R]: ssh uses every one of
those, ari keeps the first and warns.
A block without \f[B]User\f[R] stays
without one, so ssh uses whoever
connects.
Point \f[B]ssh.path\f[R] only at a file
ari owns: an Include, Match or
\f[V]Host *\f[R] in it would be gone
after the next export.
.PP
With the \f[B]ansible\f[R] module,
\f[I]SOURCE\f[R] is an inventory
directory, and every \f[B].yml\f[R] and
\f[B].yaml\f[R] file at its top level is
read.
\f[B]all.vars\f[R] become the defaults,
each host\[aq]s \f[B]ansible_host\f[R],
\f[B]ansible_user\f[R],
\f[B]ansible_port\f[R],
\f[B]ansible_ssh_private_key_file\f[R]
and \f[B]description\f[R] become its
fields, and group membership, child
groups and the file each group came from
carry over.
\f[B]ansible_host\f[R] and
\f[B]description\f[R] must be strings:
YAML reads \f[V]ansible_host: no\f[R] as
false, so a host like that is skipped
and a description like that left out,
each reported.
A quoted \f[B]ansible_port\f[R] such as
\f[V]\[dq]2222\[dq]\f[R] is the number,
as Ansible reads it.
Anything else is reported as not
imported: other vars, group vars, and
members that no hosts section defines.
Comments are read by nothing and listed
with their file and line, so zone
headers and reasons can be put back with
\f[B]ari group\f[R].
When the inventory has no
\f[B]ansible\f[R] table yet, import
prints one to paste into config.toml,
with each file\[aq]s groups listed by
name.
Export writes the hosts file with hosts
only, so groups defined there get a file
of their own in that table, named in a
warning.
The groups the source brings get the
check \f[B]group\f[R] runs: if they
would add a cycle or an undeclared
child, the problem is refused and the
inventory\[aq]s groups stay as they
were, and a host in a group that
didn\[aq]t get declared is refused with
it.
.SS export [\f[I]MODULE\f[R] ...]
.PP
Write the output of every enabled module
of every inventory, or only the named
modules, or one inventory with
\f[B]-i\f[R].
Validation and the guard check run
first; if any fails, nothing is written.
Every file is then written beside its
target before any is renamed into place,
so a write that fails, on a full disk
say, leaves every file and the guard as
they were.
A file whose contents are already right
is left alone, apart from its mode if
that differs.
.PP
.TS
tab(@);
lw(24.5n) lw(45.5n).
