that stopped it, with nothing written.
A hand-edited target stays refused;
overwriting it takes \f[B]ari export
--force\f[R]
T}
T{
\f[B]q\f[R]
T}@T{
In the list, a host\[aq]s details or an
export report, quit.
In the form, the group picker and the
delete prompt it\[aq]s an ordinary key,
so nothing unsaved is lost to it
T}
.TE
.PP
The form holds the inventory (on
\f[B]a\f[R] only), name, hostname, user,
port, key, notes, aliases separated by
spaces, ssh options one per line as
ssh_config takes them (\f[I]KEYWORD
VALUE\f[R] or
\f[I]KEYWORD\f[R]=\f[I]VALUE\f[R]), and
a box per module to exclude the host
from.
Groups aren\[aq]t on it.
An empty user, port or key follows the
inventory default, which shows in the
empty field; emptying one that\[aq]s set
goes back to the default, as
\f[B]\[aq]\[aq]\f[R] does for
\f[B]edit\f[R].
.PP
\f[B]Tab\f[R] moves to the next field,
and so does \f[B]Enter\f[R] in a
one-line field; in the ssh options it
starts a new line.
\f[B]Ctrl+S\f[R] saves and
\f[B]Escape\f[R] cancels.
Saving runs the checks \f[B]add\f[R] and
\f[B]edit\f[R] run and saves nothing if
any fails: each problem shows under the
field it\[aq]s about, and one that
belongs to no field at the top.
After a save the list reads the
inventories again with the cursor on the
host, and says so when the filter hides
it.
.PP
The group picker lists the
inventory\[aq]s declared groups in the
order they\[aq]re declared, each with
the first line of its description,
checked where the host is a member.
A group the host names without a
declaration shows too, marked as not
declared, so it can be unchecked.
\f[B]Space\f[R] checks or unchecks the
highlighted group, and leaving a group
drops its reason.
When the highlighted group is checked
and declares reasons, a reason picker
below it offers them and \f[I]no
reason\f[R]; the reason chosen shows
beside the group.
\f[B]Ctrl+S\f[R] saves what changed, as
\f[B]edit --group\f[R] and
\f[B]--ungroup\f[R] would and through
the same checks, and \f[B]Escape\f[R]
cancels.
A refusal shows above the list and saves
nothing.
Belonging to two zones passes here, as
it does for \f[B]edit\f[R];
\f[B]export\f[R] refuses it.
.SH OPTIONS
.PP
.TS
tab(@);
lw(24.5n) lw(45.5n).
T{
Option
T}@T{
Meaning
T}
_
T{
\f[B]-i\f[R], \f[B]--inventory\f[R]
\f[I]INV\f[R]
T}@T{
Inventory to act on; accepted before or
after the command
T}
T{
\f[B]-h\f[R], \f[B]--help\f[R]
T}@T{
Show help for \f[B]ari\f[R] or for one
command
T}
T{
\f[B]--version\f[R]
T}@T{
Show the version
T}
.TE
.PP
\f[B]add\f[R], \f[B]import\f[R] and
\f[B]group\f[R] \f[I]NAME\f[R] write to
one inventory: \f[B]-i\f[R], then
\f[B]ARI_INVENTORY\f[R], then
\f[B]default\f[R] from config.toml,
which is also where the TUI\[aq]s add
form starts.
The TUI, \f[B]ls\f[R], \f[B]show\f[R],
\f[B]export\f[R] and \f[B]group\f[R]
without a name cover every inventory
unless \f[B]-i\f[R] narrows them, and
\f[B]edit\f[R] and \f[B]rm\f[R] find the
host wherever it is.
An inventory \f[B]-i\f[R] names that
config.toml doesn\[aq]t declare is an
error.
.SH CONFIGURATION
.PP
\f[B]config.toml\f[R] declares the
inventories and the modules each one
uses, one table per module.
\f[B]ari init\f[R] writes a starter when
there\[aq]s none; after that
\f[B]ari\f[R] only reads it.
Only \f[B]init\f[R], \f[B]modules\f[R],
\f[B]--help\f[R] and \f[B]--version\f[R]
run without it.
.IP
.nf
\f[C]
default = \[dq]personal\[dq]

[inventories.personal.ssh]
path = \[dq]\[ti]/.ssh/config.d/10-personal.conf\[dq]

[inventories.work]
file = \[dq]\[ti]/work/infra/ari/work.json\[dq]

[inventories.work.ssh]
path = \[dq]\[ti]/.ssh/config.d/20-work.conf\[dq]

[inventories.work.ansible]
dir = \[dq]\[ti]/work/infra/ansible/inventory\[dq]
zones = \[dq]zone_*\[dq]

[inventories.work.ansible.groups]
\[dq]10-zones.yml\[dq] = [\[dq]zone_*\[dq]]
\[dq]40-roles.yml\[dq] = [\[dq]role_*\[dq], \[dq]do_not_touch\[dq]]
\[dq]70-lifecycle.yml\[dq] = [\[dq]lifecycle_*\[dq], \[dq]no_auto_update\[dq]]
\f[R]
.fi
.PP
.TS
