T}@T{
More names on the Host line
T}
T{
\f[B]user\f[R], \f[B]port\f[R]
T}@T{
Override the inventory defaults
T}
T{
\f[B]keys\f[R]
T}@T{
Declared keys ssh offers, in order,
instead of the default list
T}
T{
\f[B]notes\f[R]
T}@T{
A comment above the Host block, and the
Ansible description
T}
T{
\f[B]groups\f[R]
T}@T{
Groups the host is in, each declared
under \f[B]groups\f[R]
T}
T{
\f[B]reasons\f[R]
T}@T{
Why the host is in a group: a reason key
that group declares
T}
T{
\f[B]exclude\f[R]
T}@T{
Modules whose output leaves this host
out
T}
T{
\f[B]modules\f[R]
T}@T{
Each module\[aq]s own data, under its
name
T}
T{
\f[V]modules.ssh.options\f[R]
T}@T{
Any other ssh_config keywords, written
in order; a list for one used on every
line
T}
.TE
.PP
Every group a host uses is declared
under \f[B]groups\f[R], even as an empty
\f[V]{}\f[R], so a typo fails instead of
creating a group.
\f[B]ari group\f[R] declares and changes
them.
A declaration takes three optional keys:
\f[B]description\f[R], whose first line
is a zone\[aq]s section header in the
hosts file and whose further lines
become comments under it;
\f[B]children\f[R], the group\[aq]s
child groups; and \f[B]reasons\f[R], an
ordered map of reason key to text.
.PP
Every key a host or the defaults list is
declared under \f[B]keys\f[R], by a name
with no spaces: letters, digits and
\f[V]_ . + \[at] -\f[R], starting with a
letter, digit or \f[V]_\f[R].
A declaration holds the private
key\[aq]s \f[B]path\f[R], and
\f[B]pub\f[R], the public key, which
\f[B]ari\f[R] keeps as written but
doesn\[aq]t fill or check yet.
A host that lists keys uses those
instead of the defaults\[aq] list, not
on top of them.
.PP
Options take a string.
\f[B]LocalForward\f[R],
\f[B]RemoteForward\f[R],
\f[B]DynamicForward\f[R] and
\f[B]SendEnv\f[R] can also take a list,
since ssh uses every line of those; any
other keyword uses only its first line,
so a list there doesn\[aq]t load.
A list of one is stored as its string.
A host\[aq]s value for a keyword
replaces the default\[aq]s, lists
included.
.PP
Data for a module that isn\[aq]t
installed is kept as it is, so removing
a plugin never loses anything.
Older files are upgraded when
they\[aq]re read and saved as version 3
on their next write.
A version 1 file, from ari 0.2, moves
its ssh options and Ansible opt-out
under \f[B]modules\f[R].
A version 2 file, up to ari 0.6, keeps
one key path per host and in the
defaults; each path becomes a declared
key named after the file\[aq]s stem,
numbered when two files share one, so
every generated file stays byte for byte
the same.
.PP
A file that fails to parse stops
\f[B]ari\f[R] with the path and the
error.
It is never treated as empty.
Two ssh options that differ only in case
are one keyword to ssh, so a file that
holds both doesn\[aq]t load.
.SH VALIDATION
.PP
\f[B]add\f[R] and \f[B]edit\f[R] check
the host they write and save nothing if
any check fails, listing every problem
at once: the name and aliases are valid
ssh host names and unique across all
inventories, the hostname has no spaces,
the port is 1-65535, every key is
declared and listed once, every group is
declared, every reason is one its group
declares, and each installed module
accepts the host\[aq]s data.
ssh options can\[aq]t repeat a keyword
the host has a field for, like
\f[B]User\f[R] or \f[B]Port\f[R].
The TUI\[aq]s form and group picker save
through these same checks.
.PP
\f[B]group\f[R] checks the group it
writes the same way: a new name is
usable, every child is declared,
children form no cycle, and nothing a
host relies on is removed.
Only problems the write would add stop
it; one already elsewhere in the
inventory doesn\[aq]t.
.PP
\f[B]export\f[R] checks every inventory
it covers before writing anything.
Beyond names, keys, groups and reasons,
including the keys the defaults list,
every child group must be declared,
children may not form a cycle, and no
alias may be its host\[aq]s own name in
another case.
The \f[B]ansible\f[R] module adds its
own rules: every declared group is a
valid Ansible group name and matches
exactly one entry in the
\f[B]groups\f[R] table, and, when
\f[B]zones\f[R] is set, every host it
exports is in exactly one zone.
.SH GENERATED SSH CONFIG
.PP
Hosts are sorted by name.
Each block gets \f[B]HostName\f[R],
\f[B]User\f[R] when one is set,
\f[B]Port\f[R] when it isn\[aq]t 22, an
\f[B]IdentityFile\f[R] for each of its
keys in order, then \f[B]IdentitiesOnly
yes\f[R] once when there is a key,
followed by the host\[aq]s other
options, one line for each value of a
list.
There are no \f[V]Host *\f[R] blocks:
they ignore file boundaries, and
\f[B]IdentityFile\f[R] accumulates
across matching blocks, so a default in
one file would offer its key to every
host.
.PP
Every write goes to \f[V]FILE.tmp\f[R]
beside the target and is then renamed
over it.
\f[V]Include config.d/*.conf\f[R] never
matches the \f[V].tmp\f[R], so ssh never
reads a half-written file.
The file gets mode 0600, as do the
inventories, and a directory
\f[B]ari\f[R] creates for them gets
0700.
.SH GENERATED ANSIBLE INVENTORY
.PP
The hosts file gets \f[B]all.vars\f[R]
from the defaults, in the order
\f[B]ansible_user\f[R],
\f[B]ansible_ssh_private_key_file\f[R],
\f[B]ansible_port\f[R].
With \f[B]zones\f[R] set, hosts come in
sections, one per zone in the order the
zones are declared, each opened by a
blank line and a header comment padded
to 62 characters.
A zone without a description uses its
group name.
Within a section hosts sort by IP
address, with names that aren\[aq]t
addresses after them.
Each host gets \f[B]ansible_host\f[R],
\f[B]description\f[R] from its notes,
and \f[B]ansible_user\f[R],
\f[B]ansible_port\f[R] or
\f[B]ansible_ssh_private_key_file\f[R]
only where it differs from the defaults.
\f[B]ansible_ssh_private_key_file\f[R]
takes one file, its first key\[aq]s.
.PP
Each file in the \f[B]groups\f[R] table
holds the groups its globs match, in
declaration order, under
\f[V]all: children:\f[R], one blank line
before each group.
A group lists its children first, then
its hosts in the hosts file\[aq]s order.
Hosts without a reason come first; then
each reason, in the order its group
declares them, writes its text as a
comment followed by its hosts.
.PP
Values are written plain when YAML reads
them back unchanged, and double-quoted
otherwise.
The same inventory always produces the
same bytes, so a diff of the generated
files shows only real changes.
Files in the directory that the
\f[B]groups\f[R] table doesn\[aq]t name
are never touched.
.PP
The files get mode 0644, so anyone who
runs playbooks from the repository can
read them, and a directory \f[B]ari\f[R]
creates for them gets 0755.
A directory that already exists keeps
its mode.
.SH FILES
.PP
