.SH EXIT STATUS
.PP
\f[B]0\f[R] on success.
\f[B]1\f[R] on an error, a validation
failure, a guard refusal, or an import
conflict or refusal.
\f[B]2\f[R] on a usage error.
\f[B]130\f[R] when interrupted.
.SH EXAMPLES
.PP
Take over an existing ssh config file,
then regenerate it:
.IP
.nf
\f[C]
ari import ssh \[ti]/.ssh/config.d/10-personal.conf -i personal
ari export
\f[R]
.fi
.PP
Bring an Ansible inventory in, then the
ssh-only hosts beside it:
.IP
.nf
\f[C]
ari import ansible \[ti]/work/infra/ansible/inventory -i work
ari import ssh \[ti]/.ssh/config.d/20-work.conf -i work --exclude ansible
\f[R]
.fi
.PP
Declare a zone with its header, and a
group with a reason hosts can give:
.IP
.nf
\f[C]
ari -i work group zone_app --description \[aq]app subnet (192.0.2.0/25)\[aq]
ari -i work group no_auto_update --reason \[aq]secrets=secrets and prod path\[aq]
\f[R]
.fi
.PP
Add a host to a zone and a monitoring
group, then give it a reason to stay out
of updates:
.IP
.nf
\f[C]
ari -i work add vault-03 192.0.2.32 --group zone_app --group monitoring_db
ari edit vault-03 --group no_auto_update:secrets
ari export
\f[R]
.fi
.PP
Move a host to a new address, and retire
another:
.IP
.nf
\f[C]
ari edit vault-03 --hostname 192.0.2.33
ari rm oldbox
\f[R]
.fi
.PP
Find every host on a subnet:
.IP
.nf
\f[C]
ari ls --search 192.0.2.
\f[R]
.fi
.PP
Browse the work hosts in the TUI, then
filter with \f[B]/\f[R] and ssh to one
with \f[B]s\f[R]:
.IP
.nf
\f[C]
ari -i work
\f[R]
.fi
.PP
Accept a file after reviewing a hand
edit:
.IP
.nf
\f[C]
ari export --force
\f[R]
.fi
